# RAPL energy counters, handed to one group.
#
# /sys/class/powercap/*/energy_uj is root only on current kernels, and that is
# not an oversight: a fine grained power trace of a machine is a side channel.
# CVE-2020-8694, published as PLATYPUS, recovered AES-NI keys and defeated
# KASLR from unprivileged reads of exactly these counters. The kernel's answer
# was to take the read away from everyone but root.
#
# This rule hands it back to one group. Whatever that group can run can watch
# the machine's power draw at whatever rate it likes - which on a single user
# desktop is the applet and the browser it is displaying, and on a shared
# machine is every one of the group's members. Install it only if that trade
# is one you want to make; nothing else in the applet needs it, and without it
# the package power row is simply not shown.
#
#   sudo make install-rapl                     - group adm
#   sudo make install-rapl RAPL_GROUP=powermon - some group of your own
#   sudo make uninstall-rapl                   - remove this access rule and
#                                                 reapply remaining udev policy
#
# %p is the device's path under /sys, so this names the counter of whichever
# domain the event was for: the package, and each of the domains inside it.
#
# udev runs RUN+= without a PATH lookup, so both commands are named absolutely.
# Where coreutils lives is not the same on every distribution, so the installer
# resolves each one on this machine and fails rather than shipping a rule whose
# every run would only be a line in the udev log.
ACTION!="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="powercap", KERNEL=="*-rapl:*", \
    RUN+="@CHGRP@ -- @GROUP@ /sys%p/energy_uj", \
    RUN+="@CHMOD@ 0440 /sys%p/energy_uj"
